# inv-patch: 29-l5-gring
# purpose: L5 oracle (H12, the I5 S2b cells), a global per-poll-group Mode D record ring: an upper bound on what one shared completion stream per host poll group buys over per-qpair record rings (built on 20's trfree records, w0 = req). Two host modes, the same device: IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING=1 = per-qpair poll calls retained, it isolates the per-qpair ring probe and batching; IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING=2 = idle per-qpair calls skipped, it bounds per-queue polling elimination. H12 is read by comparing the q1 -> q8 slopes of l1 (SPDK_INV_TRFREE=1 alone), gring1 and gring2 from the same inv-gring binary; flatness is not assumed. Host: with IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING=1|2 every trfree Mode D IO qpair (tail byte inv_gring_on, set at the end of construct; no inv_flags bit) joins its poll group's ring at connect. The first member allocates IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING_ENTRIES (E, default 4096) x 32 B of zeroed DMA memory exactly as cq_rec_ring is allocated (struct inv_gring {ring, iova, head, phase = 1, entries, members, inflight_cap, budget, consumed} at the tail of struct nvme_pcie_poll_group; each member keeps a pointer to it at the qpair tail), and every member MMIO-writes the ring IOVA to CQ_REC_RING_IOVA_OFFSET + 8*qid on its own controller. Registration order relied on (perf: spdk_nvme_ctrlr_alloc_io_qpair with create_only, spdk_nvme_poll_group_add, spdk_nvme_ctrlr_connect_io_qpair; spdk_nvme_poll_group_add only accepts a DISCONNECTED qpair, so a qpair is always in its group before connect): construct writes the qpair's own record ring IOVA (stock); the generic layer sets qpair->poll_group; connect (_nvme_pcie_ctrlr_create_io_qpair) joins and rewrites the register BEFORE it submits CREATE IO CQ; nvmf_tgt reads the register only when it handles CREATE IO SQ (sent from the CQ completion) and no IO can be submitted before the SQ exists, so the device writes every record of a gring qpair to the shared ring from the first IO on. nvme_pcie_poll_group_process_completions tests one poll-group field (inv_gring.ring != NULL) and diverts through a cold one-jmp trampoline (as 20 does for the per-qpair poll) to inv_gring_poll_group (noinline, hot; one instantiation per mode, dispatched once per call on inv_gring.mode): the stock disconnected-qpair callbacks, then ONE in-order consume of the shared ring per call (at most the members' summed max_completions_cap, or completions_per_qpair per member when smaller; per record: w3 phase test, w0 = req, req->qpair -> pqpair, the cid pushed on that qpair's 20 stack, queue_depth--, a per-qpair credit inv_gring_done++, req->cb_fn(w1, &cpl), nvme_free_request), then the stock per-qpair loop (mode 2 skips the spdk_nvme_qpair_process_completions call of a member for which it would be a no-op, inv_gring_idle: no credit, last_sq_desc_tail == sq_desc_tail (no batched SQ doorbell pending), queued_req, aborting_queued_req and err_req_head empty, state ENABLED with transport_failure_reason NONE (nvme_qpair_check_enabled then has no side effect: it acts only on a CONNECTED qpair or on an ENABLED one whose failure reason a reset set), pcie_state READY, no deferred delete, no pending vtophys failure, controller not failed, no timeout callback; a member with a credit, a pending doorbell or queued work is called exactly as in mode 1), in which inv_process_completions returns a gring qpair's credit (and zeroes it) without reading its own record ring, so the stock poll tail (stats, batched SQ doorbell), nvme_qpair_resubmit_requests and the poll-group return value (perf's busy/idle tsc split) still see the completions (returning 0 there, as the plan text says, would starve resubmission and count every poll idle). Flow control: the device may write ordinal k only once the host consumed k - E; every unconsumed ordinal is an in-flight IO of a member, bounded per qpair by inv_gring_cap = min(cid stack, io_queue_requests + 1) (the request pool, enforced by the library), so a join fails with -ENOTSUP when the members' bounds would sum past E (perf: 3 x ceil(QD/qpairs) + 1 per qpair, e.g. 8 x 193 = 1544 for QD 512 on 8 qpairs, min(896, 1537) = 896 for QD 512 on 1 qpair; the num_entries sum would be 8 x 1024 > 4096 and reject q8). Target: with IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING=1 or 2 (identical on the device; read once at transport create by iau_inv_gring_init) every Mode D SQ maps E x 32 B at its record ring IOVA and takes a reference on a process-wide table entry keyed by that IOVA (64 entries under a mutex, not the plan's 4: perf's default association makes max(app cores, controllers) poll groups, e.g. 8 rings for 8 app cores x 2 controllers, so 64 is ample; an entry is created with ordinal 0 by the first SQ and released by the last; one "INV_GRING target: iova=0x.. entries=E" line per entry); iau_flush tests one static flag after 23's size test and tail-calls iau_inv_flush_gring, which reserves the staged count of consecutive ordinals with one __atomic_fetch_add (ordinal k: slot k % E, phase 1 ^ ((k / E) & 1), so the first pass writes phase 1 as the host expects), writes w0..w2, spdk_wmb(), then w3 per record, and keeps the per-SQ credit accounting. Ordering caveat: producers on different device threads can publish ordinal k+1 before k; the host consumes strictly in order and waits on k (head-of-line), which can only add latency, never reorder or lose records; acceptable for a bound. With both envs unset nvme_pcie_qpair_submit_request and nvme_pcie_qpair_process_completions compile to the same instructions (only __LINE__ immediates differ), nvme_pcie_poll_group_process_completions gains one cmpq of the poll-group field + one not-taken jne (and a register renaming), iau_flush one rip-relative cmpb + one not-taken jne (plus a loop-alignment nop), and stock struct offsets are unchanged (new fields at the tails)
# safety: UNSAFE
# breaks: abort, timeout, reset and teardown of in-flight IO (20's, plus: a record of IO still in flight when its qpair left the group or was freed is still consumed by the group loop and touches that qpair; the direct nvme_pcie_qpair_process_completions call that nvme_pcie_ctrlr_delete_io_qpair makes after DELETE IO SQ only returns credits and does not drain the shared ring; the group loop does not run in the qpair's completion context, so a callback that frees its qpair is not deferred; reconnect rewrites only the shared ring IOVA, and when the last SQ of a ring is deleted on the target (every member reconnecting, or a controller reset) the target frees its table entry and recreates it at ordinal 0 on the next attach while the host ring keeps its head and phase: the ordinals desync and perf hangs); gring qpairs complete only through spdk_nvme_poll_group_process_completions (polling one directly returns credits and never consumes the ring); requires SPDK_INV_TRFREE=1 (LOCKED 32 B records, w0 = req): with IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING=1|2 a Mode D IO qpair that is not trfree, or has SPDK_INV_THIN, IAU_INV_REC_BYTES=16|64, IAU_INV_DESC_BYTES, SPDK_INV_ADDR or SPDK_INV_PF, or Mode E, fails construct with -ENOTSUP (nvmf_tgt treats every Mode D record ring as shared, so there is no stock fallback; mode 0 ignores the env; any value other than 1 or 2 is off on both sides); per-poll-group ring only: a gring qpair connected outside a poll group fails connect with -ENOTSUP, and a group's qpairs must all be served by one nvmf_tgt process (the producer counter is per process; perf has one poll group per worker and namespace, so one controller per ring); IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING_ENTRIES outside 1..65536 fails construct (host) and every Mode D SQ create (target); a join that would take the group's in-flight bound past E fails connect with -ENOTSUP (raise E on both sides); nvmf_tgt and the host must see the same IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING and IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING_ENTRIES (run_point exports IAU_INV_* to both): a disagreement corrupts host memory or hangs perf, and check_l.py FAILs a point whose host and target entries or IOVAs differ; at most 64 rings per nvmf_tgt process (more fail SQ create with -ENOSPC), and one host process per nvmf_tgt (the table is keyed by IOVA = host VA on vfio-user, so two host processes could collide); the target needs 32 B records (IAU_INV_REC_BYTES 16|64 fails SQ create); per-IO cycle records are not taken; polls and idle_polls count per-qpair calls (mode 2 does not count the skipped ones); oracle overhead the design would not pay: the poll-group field test (on the group's third cache line, otherwise cold in the stock path), one inv_gring_on test per poll in the lever loop (also paid by the 20 arm), the per-qpair credit increment per completion, one flag test per target flush and the 64-bit modulo and division per target flush (ordinal to slot and phase), and in mode 2 the inv_gring_idle predicate per member per poll; the cross-thread fetch-add contention on the shared counter and the 64 B lines two producers' 32 B records share are part of what the bound measures. §4 L evidence: host "INV_GRING ring=P iova=I entries=E mode=M" per ring at creation, "INV_GRING qid=N joined entries=E ring=P cap=K group_cap=G" per IO qpair at connect, "INV_GRING ring=P consumed=C members=M at destroy" per ring at poll-group destroy (the cid balance stays 20's "INV_TRFREE qid=N all K cids returned at destroy" line, not duplicated); target "INV_GRING target: iova=I entries=E" per ring; check_l.py expects one join line per IO qpair (qpairs x n_ctx, n_ctx = max(app cores, controllers): perf's default ns/worker association, one poll group per ns_ctx), one ring line (mode = the env value) and one destroy line (C > 0, M = 0) per poll group (n_ctx), every join's ring among the ring lines, E on every line, target IOVAs equal to the host ring IOVAs, and the sum of C equal to the sum of emitted= on the target's IAU_STATS lines; gring adds no inv_flags bit (INV_FLAGS stays 0x01)
# env: IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING=1|2 (1 = per-qpair poll calls retained, 2 = idle per-qpair calls skipped; the device is the same for both) and IAU_INV_GLOBAL_RING_ENTRIES=<E> (default 4096, 1..65536), read by BOTH nvmf_tgt (transport create) and the host (at load, inv_env_prime); requires SPDK_INV_TRFREE=1 on the host
